<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jhansi State</h1><p class="st-meta">1804 CE – 1858 CE (approx.) · capital Jhansi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Maratha state of Jhansi in Bundelkhand, annexed under the doctrine of lapse and made famous by Rani Lakshmibai's resistance in 1857–58.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jhansi was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundela">Bundelas</a> in the 18th century and held by governors appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>, the Newalkar family. In 1804 the governor made a treaty with the British, and after the Peshwa's fall in 1817 the family became hereditary rulers of a protected state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Newalkar Rajas governed Jhansi fort and its surrounding plain, a strategic point between the Gangetic plain and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao">Gangadhar Rao</a> adopted a son shortly before his death in 1853, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> refused to recognise the adoption.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The British annexed Jhansi under the doctrine of lapse in 1854. In 1857 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmibai">Rani Lakshmibai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangadhar-rao">Gangadhar Rao's</a> widow, took control, and she fought the British until she was killed near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a> in June 1858. Jhansi was then absorbed into British territory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jhansi-state.jpg" alt="Jhansi State" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Princely_State_of_Jhansi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1804 CE – 1858 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jhansi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gangadhar Rao, Lakshmibai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bundeli, Marathi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jhansi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1831&amp;focus=jhansi-state" aria-label="Jhansi State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6761 1241 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jhansi State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7081 1497L6991 1757M7081 1497L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6991 1757h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7081 1497h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7081 1497h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1831&amp;focus=jhansi-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

